<ArticleLayout slug="agency-white-label-question" ctaLabel="Start a white-label brief" faq={faq}>
  <p class="standfirst">An agency that sells production under its own name signs two contracts, one with its client and one with its supplier. The protections that matter have to be written into both, because the one published US production standard read for this page assumes the supplier knows who the advertiser is.</p>

  <h2>How does white-label creative production for agencies work?</h2>

  <p>It runs on two contracts: the agency's contract with its client and the agency's contract with its supplier. The NDA, the contact rules, ownership of the work and any trained model, the margin and AI disclosure all have to line up across both. hubStudio works on the supply side of that arrangement.</p>

  <p>This page describes production practice, not legal advice. A lawyer drafts both contracts, and nothing here says what a court would enforce.</p>

