  <p>The conversation that comes before this paper is covered in the <a href="/resources/insights/questions-to-ask-ai-production-partner">questions to ask an AI production partner article</a>. This page starts where the talking stops.</p>

  <h2>What should a white-label production NDA cover?</h2>

  <p>Sign it first. Guidance from WIPO and from the US commercial production trade body both put the NDA at the very start of a supplier relationship.</p>

  <blockquote class="evidence">WIPO's trade secrets guide sets out the drafting points for an NDA: clearly identify which information is confidential, state the authorized use and the specific purposes, set out the exceptions, and regulate what happens to confidential documents once they have served their purpose, for example by requiring the recipient to "return or destroy the information and provide certification of the destruction." With partners, customers and suppliers, it says to enter into the NDA "at the very beginning of the business relationship, i.e., when starting negotiations."</blockquote>
  <p class="source">Source: World Intellectual Property Organization, WIPO Guide to Trade Secrets and Innovation, first published 2024, part IV on trade secret management; guide chapter read in full in September 2026.</p>

  <blockquote class="evidence">The US commercial production trade body's bidding guide puts the NDA on the first call. Its opening checklist asks: "Has a Mutual NDA been provided for this project? (if not, the production/post company should ask for one to protect its IP)". The same checklist asks: "May the production/post company use the finished spot on its reel/socials with full credit?"</blockquote>
  <p class="source">Source: the US commercial production trade body's bidding reference guide, 2026 edition; first-call checklist read in full.</p>

  <p>That second question matters more in white-label work than anywhere else. A reel is where an invisible arrangement becomes visible.</p>

  <p>Start the agreement from its purpose. The UK Intellectual Property Office tells the disclosing party to restrict use to a specific permitted purpose, stated as precisely as it can, because a purpose can be widened later but never narrowed. In white-label production the purpose is tight: named deliverables, for one agency, for one end client.</p>

  <p>The office's own example agreements, one-way and mutual, run to nine short clauses. They make a sound skeleton. The office is plain that an IP attorney or solicitor should draw up the agreement you actually sign. The checklist below is production practice built on that skeleton, not a signable document.</p>

