  <p>Duration is a field the parties set, not an industry standard.</p>

  <blockquote class="evidence">On how long confidentiality should last, the same guidance says: "It's common to see it limited to 3 or 5 years. After that time they will be able to use and disclose your information."</blockquote>
  <p class="source">Source: UK Intellectual Property Office, non-disclosure agreements guidance, first published March 2015; a statement of common practice by the office, not a survey.</p>

  <p>Confidentiality also runs both ways. The US production trade body's guidelines say it should be mutual, and that it should never be so restrictive that the parties cannot do their jobs. A supplier's methods deserve the same cover as an agency's client list.</p>

  <h2>Can a white-label studio contact my client?</h2>

  <p>Only as far as the supplier contract allows. No trade body guideline, model contract or government source read for this page publishes a rule that a supplier may not contact the end client. The rule people repeat is a commercial term. The agency has to write it.</p>

  <p>The one production standard read in full points the other way. Its standard agreement names the advertiser, the agency and the production company on its first page, and makes agency and advertiser jointly and severally liable. Its guidelines go further when a bill is at risk.</p>

  <blockquote class="evidence">The trade body's guidelines point away from invisibility when payment is at risk. Where an agency keeps a sequential liability clause and the production company has neither a direct-payment provision nor full funding in advance, the guidelines say the production company should "Be provided with the advertiser billing and contact information," "Copy the advertiser on all invoices," and notify the advertiser of payment due as soon as the agency misses a payment date.</blockquote>
  <p class="source">Source: the US commercial production trade body's national guidelines for live action production, revised April 2020; guideline document read in full.</p>

  <p>That is the disclosed model. The advertiser is named on the supplier's paper, shares liability for the bill, and becomes the supplier's fallback when an agency pays late.</p>

  <p>White-label takes the advertiser out of the supplier's view. The fallback goes with it.</p>

  <p>So whatever the disclosed model supplies by default now has to be written into the supplier contract: payment terms, contact rules, and the two defaults in the same standard form that bar assignment without written consent and give the client, the production company and the director a revocable license to use finished work for promotion until the client says otherwise in writing.</p>

  <p>A studio's published policy can set the contact rule from its side. One example:</p>

  <blockquote class="evidence">hubStudio's agencies page describes its white-label arrangement this way: "Your clients never know we exist. You handle all client communication, strategy and relationship management. We execute the production work and deliver finished assets directly to you, under the agency brand. Clients see only your agency and your processes." That is the studio's published policy, not an industry standard.</blockquote>
  <p class="source">Source: hubStudio agencies solutions page, read September 2026; first-party statement of policy.</p>

  <p>A published policy is not a clause. The table below sets out contact rules as terms the agency writes into its supplier contract. It is production practice, not an industry standard.</p>

