  <p>A non-solicitation line is only as strong as the law that governs the contract. One statute shows how far a restraint rule can reach.</p>

  <blockquote class="evidence">California's Business and Professions Code section 16600 states that "every contract by which anyone is restrained from engaging in a lawful profession, trade, or business of any kind is to that extent void." The section, as last amended effective 1 January 2024, adds that it "shall not be limited to contracts where the person being restrained from engaging in a lawful profession, trade, or business is a party to the contract."</blockquote>
  <p class="source">Source: California Business and Professions Code section 16600, amended by Stats. 2023, Ch. 828 (AB 1076), effective January 2024; statute text read on the legislature's code display in September 2026.</p>

  <p>How that section, or any other law, treats a clause between two businesses is a question for counsel in the governing jurisdiction. This page does not answer it.</p>

  <p>One more line to draw. A referral is not a white-label buy, and the contact rules invert.</p>

  <blockquote class="evidence">hubStudio's partner program is a different arrangement. A partner makes an introduction, and hubStudio then handles "Initial discovery calls and needs assessment," "Custom proposals tailored to client needs," "Full project execution and delivery" and "Client relationship management."</blockquote>
  <p class="source">Source: hubStudio partner program page, read September 2026; first-party program description, commission terms excluded.</p>

  <p>Under a referral, the studio holds the client. Under white-label, the agency does. The <a href="/partner-program">partner program page</a> and the <a href="/solutions/agencies">agencies solutions page</a> describe the two separately, and a contract should never blend them. The <a href="/hub4you">hub4you platform page</a> covers the briefing and review side, which is where the login row above earns its place.</p>

  <h2>Who owns work made under a white-label agreement?</h2>

  <p>Not simply whoever paid. Ownership passes along a chain, supplier to agency to client, and each link is a separate contract with its own payment condition.</p>

  <p>US law sets a narrow default. A commissioned work counts as a work made for hire only if it falls in one of nine categories, among them part of a motion picture or other audiovisual work, a compilation and a contribution to a collective work.</p>

  <blockquote class="evidence">The Office lists four criteria a commissioned work must meet: it falls within one of the nine categories, there is a written agreement, the agreement expressly says the work is made for hire, and all parties sign it. "If a work fails to satisfy any of these requirements, it is not a work made for hire." Ordinarily, the Office notes, the author is the person who actually created the work.</blockquote>
  <p class="source">Source: US Copyright Office, Circular 30, Works Made for Hire, revised August 2024; the Office's circular read in full.</p>

  <p>China starts from the opposite end, which matters when the supplier works there.</p>

  <blockquote class="evidence">Under China's Copyright Law, ownership of a commissioned work is set by the contract, and where the contract is not explicit or there is no contract, copyright belongs to the commissioned party. A transfer of economic rights requires a written contract.</blockquote>
  <p class="source">Source: the Copyright Law of the People's Republic of China as amended 11 November 2020, in force 1 June 2021, Articles 19 and 27; statute text logged in the source ledger on 10 September 2026.</p>

  <p>Two jurisdictions, opposite defaults, one practical answer: write it down at both links. Neither instrument says what a court would make of a particular contract, and this page does not either.</p>

  <p>Generated material adds a layer. The US Copyright Office's 2023 guidance holds that copyright protects only human creativity, so where a system produced the expressive elements, the contract is what allocates them.</p>

  <p>Then comes payment.</p>

  <blockquote class="evidence">The US standard production agreement vests "all rights, title and interest in and to the commercial(s) which are the subject of this Agreement, including all copyrights therein as well as in and to all the exposed negatives, positives, out-takes and clips" in the contracting client, and separately provides that "Ownership of the commercial does not transfer until full payment is made to Producer."</blockquote>
  <p class="source">Source: the US commercial production trade body's Standard Commercial Production Agreement, form revised December 2007, sections 5 and 12; standard form read in full.</p>

  <p>Stack that condition across two contracts. The agency cannot hand its client rights it has not yet paid its supplier for, so the transfer date in the client contract should follow the one in the supplier contract, never run ahead of it.</p>

  <p>Trained models are where the published paper runs out.</p>

  <blockquote class="evidence">That standard agreement has no field for a trained model, model weights, a training set or prompts. No published model agreement assigning ownership of a trained model between a brand, an agency and a supplier was found in this research.</blockquote>
  <p class="source">Source: the US commercial production trade body's standard production agreement, revised December 2007, read in full for such fields on 10 September 2026, plus a review of four buyer searches the same day; none present.</p>

  <blockquote class="evidence">On training, the agreement is short: the producer may not use, or authorize any other party to use, the results and proceeds of services rendered under the contracts to train generative AI systems without the union's consent.</blockquote>
  <p class="source">Source: 2025 Commercials Contract Memorandum of Agreement, Section XX.1.A.4, agreement text, May 2025.</p>

  <p>That clause binds only signatories to the US performers' union commercials agreement. It is also the one published contract term on training found anywhere in this research, which says how thin the record is.</p>

  <p>Studio policy fills some of the gap, and shows where it stops.</p>

  <blockquote class="evidence">hubStudio publishes that "The custom models we train on your brand are your property, not ours," and that every asset is documented with "which model was used, which references trained it, which prompts produced it." In a white-label engagement the studio's client is the agency, so whether the end brand holds that model depends on the agency's own contract with the brand.</blockquote>
  <p class="source">Source: hubStudio AI excellence page, read September 2026; first-party statement of policy.</p>

  <p>A model promise made to the agency reaches the brand only if the client contract carries it forward. The table below maps each item across both contracts. Its last column restates the instruments above and predicts no outcome.</p>

