  <h2>How do agencies set margin on outsourced production?</h2>

  <p>Each company sets its own. Nobody publishes a figure with a method behind it.</p>

  <blockquote class="evidence">No method-stated figure for agency markup on outsourced production was found. Across four buyer searches and 31 results, every markup percentage in circulation was published by a seller of white-label services or agency software, with no sample and no method. The US commercial production trade body's own guidelines decline to set a percentage, and the US advertiser trade body's compensation reports could not be read at source.</blockquote>
  <p class="source">Source: search-results audit and document review run 10 September 2026, four buyer phrasings, publisher type recorded for every result, no domain named.</p>

  <p>What the record does offer is structure. On the US bid form, the production company's margin is a named line: a single markup percentage applied to the cost sections A to K, producing a production fee that covers administrative and sales costs plus a fee for services from prep through wrap. The trade body stops short of the number.</p>

  <blockquote class="evidence">The same guidelines decline to set the number: "The AICP stresses that the use of the AICP bid form (in whole or in part), fee or percentage amounts, business costs and components, and the terms of bid submissions or contract terms, are strictly a matter of independent determination by each production company."</blockquote>
  <p class="source">Source: the US commercial production trade body's national guidelines for live action production, revised April 2020; guideline document read in full.</p>

  <p>The same guidelines add an overage rule. When an agency or advertiser changes the specification and costs run over, the fee on those costs uses the markup percentage of the awarded bid. Agree that rule with the supplier, then mirror it with the client.</p>

  <p>Next, pick the buying method. It decides who carries an overrun.</p>

  <blockquote class="evidence">The standard agreement defines two buying methods. In a cost-plus job, "A fixed fee is added to the total of the costs of all the elements, which total becomes the contract price." In a firm bid, the producer submits "a bid identifying direct costs, production fee, and director's fee," and once the bid is accepted "it becomes the contract price for the job."</blockquote>
  <p class="source">Source: the US commercial production trade body's Standard Commercial Production Agreement, form revised December 2007, section 8; standard form read in full.</p>

  <p>A markup and a commission can also describe the same money. The conversion below is arithmetic, not a going rate.</p>

  <blockquote class="evidence">A markup and a commission can describe the same money from opposite ends. A joint advertiser and agency trade body compensation guide gives the conversion in its glossary, "17.65% markup is equivalent to a 15% commission," because 15 is 17.65 percent of 85.</blockquote>
  <p class="source">Source: the joint US advertiser and agency trade body compensation guide, 2006, glossary entry for mark-up, read from a third-party-hosted copy in September 2026; shown as arithmetic only, never as a current or typical rate.</p>

