        <tr>
          <th scope="row">Margin</th>
          <td>What remains after the lines above</td>
          <td>The agency's to set, and to disclose or not under the client contract</td>
        </tr>
      </tbody>
    </table>
  </div>

  <p>The last decision sits in the client contract: whether the markup, and any rebate, must be disclosed. Advertisers have not always asked.</p>

  <blockquote class="evidence">Fewer than half of the advertisers surveyed required their agency to disclose whether a production job was being bid to an in-house or affiliated production company.</blockquote>
  <p class="source">Source: US advertiser trade body production transparency study, August 2017, built on a task force of 30 member executives and 12 subject-matter organizations plus a supporting member survey whose respondent count was not published.</p>

  <blockquote class="evidence">More than 60 percent of the advertisers surveyed either did not require, or did not know whether their agency contract required, production rebates and other incentives to be passed back to them.</blockquote>
  <p class="source">Source: US advertiser trade body production transparency study, August 2017, same survey, respondent count not published.</p>

  <p>Both figures date from 2017. They show that disclosure is a term somebody writes, not a default anyone can assume.</p>

  <h2>Do my clients need to know AI was used?</h2>

  <p>Between agency and client, the trade bodies for both sides say yes. Toward consumers, only where the AI use is material.</p>

  <blockquote class="evidence">The UK agency and advertiser trade bodies' principles for generative AI say: "Advertisers and agencies should be transparent with each other about their use of AI. Neither should include AI-generated content in materials provided to the other without the other's agreement."</blockquote>
  <p class="source">Source: the UK agency trade body and the UK advertiser trade body, advertising industry principles for the use of generative AI in creative advertising, October 2023, launched 1 November 2023; all twelve principles read in full.</p>

  <p>Those principles address advertisers and agencies. They say nothing about a supplier behind the agency. Applying the same rule one link down is the reader's extension, not the principles' text. It is a reasonable one: an agency cannot be open about AI use it was never told about.</p>

  <blockquote class="evidence">A US digital advertising trade body's disclosure framework takes a risk-based approach: "Disclosure is required only when AI materially affects authenticity, identity, or representation in ways that could mislead consumers." Under it, routine production tasks, background AI tools and clearly stylized creative can proceed without disclosure.</blockquote>
  <p class="source">Source: a US digital advertising trade body's AI transparency and disclosure framework, January 2026; the framework's public landing page read in full, with the full framework behind a member login.</p>

  <p>Version 2 of that framework, announced in August 2026, says state and international regulation takes precedence over it. In the UK, the advertising regulator's guidance keeps the advertiser responsible for any AI output in its ads.</p>

  <p>China is specific about the file itself. China's Measures for Labeling of AI-Generated Synthetic Content, issued March 2025, set out two labels.</p>

  <blockquote class="evidence">The Measures require two labels on a generated image or video: an explicit label a person can perceive, and an implicit label written into the file metadata carrying the content attribute, the service provider's name or code and a content identifier. Where a provider offers download, copy or export, it must ensure the exported file still contains a conforming explicit label.</blockquote>
  <p class="source">Source: the instrument, Articles 4 and 5, in force 1 September 2025.</p>

  <p>Who carries that duty between brand, agency and studio is generally treated as a question for the contracts and for counsel. This page does not settle it. The <a href="/resources/insights/china-ai-labeling-rules-production-workflow">China AI labeling in the production workflow article</a> follows labels through a pipeline, and the <a href="/resources/insights/disclosure-audit-trail-per-asset">disclosure audit trail per asset article</a> covers the record behind them.</p>

  <p>Lines to put in both contracts, as production practice:</p>

  <p>- The supplier reports, per asset, where AI was used in making it. - The supplier delivers the provenance record with the asset: model, references, prompts. - The agency tells the client before AI-generated content enters the client's materials. - Who applies each market's label is agreed in writing before delivery. - Client material never trains a shared or public model. - A named human reviewer signs off each asset before delivery.</p>

  <h2>Questions agencies ask about white-label production</h2>

  <h3>What should a white-label production NDA cover?</h3>

  <p>The permitted purpose, what counts as confidential (including the client's identity and the arrangement itself), exclusions, onward disclosure only on equivalent terms, return or destruction of copies, no license to intellectual property, reel and portfolio use, a term and a governing law. Sign it before the first brief. A lawyer drafts it; this is production practice, not legal advice.</p>

  <h3>Can a white-label studio contact my client?</h3>

  <p>Only if your supplier contract allows it. No trade body guideline or model contract read for this page sets a rule against it, and US production trade body guidelines tell suppliers to contact the advertiser when an agency's payment is at risk. Write the rules in: what is allowed, what needs written consent, and what never happens.</p>

  <h3>Who owns work made under a white-label agreement?</h3>

  <p>Whoever the two contracts say, once the payments clear. In the US a commissioned work is a work made for hire only in nine categories and only with a signed written agreement. Under China's Copyright Law, a silent contract leaves copyright with the commissioned party. No published agreement assigning a trained model was found, so write that down too.</p>

  <h3>How do agencies set margin on outsourced production?</h3>

  <p>Each company sets its own. No method-stated markup figure for outsourced production was found, and the US production trade body leaves percentages to each company's independent determination. Build the price from supplier cost, agency handling, revision exposure and chain risk, then choose cost-plus or a firm bid and decide what the client contract must disclose.</p>

  <h3>Do my clients need to know AI was used?</h3>

  <p>UK agency and advertiser trade bodies ask both sides to be transparent with each other about AI use, and to agree before AI content goes into the other's materials. A US digital advertising framework asks for consumer disclosure only where AI materially affects authenticity, identity or representation. Who carries a label under China's rules is generally treated as a question for the contracts.</p>
